  "strategy": "### Approach and Rationale\nWe aim to minimize the score \\( \\frac{2n \\cdot \\max(\\text{convolve}(a, \\text{mode}='full'))}{\\sum_{i} a_i^2} \\) using a multi-level exploration strategy. Unlike the previous approach that directly refines sequences at their nominal lengths, we will dynamically adjust the length of the sequence during optimization. This is motivated by the observation that sequences of different lengths might have different optimal distributions, and by potentially finding that a slightly different length yields a better score.\n\n### Mathematical Specification\nLet \\( a \\) be a sequence of non-negative coefficients (normalized so that \\( \\sum_{i} a_i \\geq 0.01 \\) and bounded by [0, 1000]). For any \\( a \\) of length \\( n \\), the score is computed as:\n\\[\n\\text{score} = \\frac{2n \\cdot \\max(\\text{full convolution}(a))}{\\left(\\sum_{i} a_i\\right)^2}\n\\]\nTo dynamically adjust the length, we will:\n1. Define candidate lengths \\( \\{ 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048 \\} \\)\n2. Initialize sequences for each candidate length using cubic interpolation of the parent sequence if available, or uniformly if not.\n3. Use the subgradient method from the previous program to refine sequences at their respective lengths.\n\n### Implementation Procedure\n1. **Initialization**:\n   - If the parent sequence is available (from `height_sequence_1`), cubic interpolate it to target lengths.\n   - Otherwise, use uniformly distributed sequences normalized to sum 1.\n   - Compute initial scores for all candidate sequences.\n\n2. **Optimization Loop**:\n   - Use a multi-phased optimization schedule that initially uses the parent sequence and shorter candidate sequences.\n   - For each candidate length, perform a local optimization (using the subgradient method) to find the best sequence.\n   - Compare these best sequences and update the global best solution accordingly.\n\n3. **Phase Transition**:\n   - The optimization process will explore sequences of different lengths independently.\n\n4. **Fallback**:\n   - If the best solution has an infinite score, return a uniform fallback sequence of length 768 (normalized).\n\n### Search Choices and Useful Variations\nWe will explore candidate sequences from lengths \\( \\{ 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048 \\} \\). Initialization is done via cubic interpolation if the parent is available, to preserve the parent's structure. Otherwise, uniform sequences are used. The core optimization uses the subgradient method from the previous program, but with a crucial modification: we optimize sequences at fixed lengths and then compare the results across lengths.\n\n### Computational Budget and Robustness\nTotal time: 1800 seconds.\n- **Length Exploration**:\n  - Short lengths first (to reduce initial convolution costs).\n  - Remaining time for optimization of each length.\n\n### Validation and Return Contract\nEvaluate all candidate sequences using the provided `evaluate_sequence` function. Only sequences yielding a finite score will be considered. It must be normalized (sum \u2265 0.01) before evaluation.\n\nLet's implement this strategy accordingly.",
